l. ", "type": "text"}], "id": 1, "selected": 0, "correct": false}], "_data": [{"contents": [{"content": "", "type": "text"}], "type": "question"}, {"content": "secondary_1", "type": "objective"}], "id": 0}, {"responses": [{"_data": [{"content": "The concept of the Turing Machine represents the essence of computer programming ", "type": "text"}], "id": 0, "selected": 0, "correct": true}, {"_data": [{"content": "because it stated many of the computer functions. ", "type": "text"}], "id": 1, "selected": 0, "correct": false}], "_data": [{"contents": [{"content": "", "type": "text"}], "type": "question"}, {"content": "secondary_1", "type": "objective"}], "id": 1}, {"responses": [{"_data": [{"content": "The Turing Machine operates when it processes", "type": "text"}], "id": 0, "selected": 0, "correct": true}, {"_data": [{"content": "a series of mathematical symbols. ", "type": "text"}], "id": 1, "selected": 0, "correct": false}], "_data": [{"contents": [{"content": "", "type": "text"}], "type": "question"}, {"content": "secondary_1", "type": "objective"}], "id": 2}]}, {"template_override": 1, "_data": [{"contents": [], "type": "instructions"}, {"contents": [{"content": "Quiz", "type": "group_name"}], "type": "group"}, {"content": {"incorrect_feedback": [{"content": "Whoops! Something important is missing.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Maybe next time...", "type": "text"}], "allowed_attempts": 2, "attempt_feedback": [{"content": "", "type": "text"}, {"content": "", "type": "text"}], "correct_feedback": "Correct!"}, "type": "feedback"}], "type": "RSLT", "stimuli_ids": [], "id": 11, "questions": [{"responses": [{"_data": [{"content": "", "type": "text"}], "id": 0, "selected": 0, "correct": 0}, {"_data": [{"content": "", "type": "text"}], "id": 1, "selected": 0, "correct": 1}], "_data": [{"contents": [{"content": "", "type": "text"}], "type": "question"}], "id": 0}]}, {"template_override": 1, "_data": [{"contents": [{"content": " Let's check inventions derived from Turing Machine concept. ", "type": "text"}], "type": "instructions"}, {"contents": [{"content": "Take Away", "type": "group_name"}], "type": "group"}, {"content": {"incorrect_feedback": [{"content": "", "type": "text"}, {"content": "", "type": "text"}], "allowed_attempts": 2, "attempt_feedback": [{"content": "", "type": "text"}, {"content": "", "type": "text"}], "correct_feedback": "You're done!"}, "type": "feedback"}, {"contents": [{"content": "Reading", "type": "skill"}, {"content": "Writing", "type": "skill"}, {"content": "Speaking", "type": "skill"}], "type": "learning_skills"}, {"contents": [{"content": "Instructions: Complete the following steps. Fill in the Self-Assessment.", "type": "text"}], "type": "post_instructions"}], "type": "DWS", "stimuli_ids": [], "id": 12, "questions": [{"responses": [{"_data": [{"content": "", "type": "text"}], "id": 0, "selected": 0, "correct": 1}], "_data": [{"contents": [{"content": "enpe7n1le02ob03re7pdf01.pdf", "type": "pdf"}], "type": "question"}], "id": 0}]}], "_data": [{"contents": [{"content": true, "type": "has_nav"}, {"content": true, "type": "has_exercise_controls"}], "type": "lab_options"}], "id": 0, "stimuli": [{"template_override": 1, "contents": [{}], "_data": [], "id": 0, "type": "welcome"}, {"template_override": 1, "contents": [{"value": {"images": [{"id": 0, "image": "enpe7n1le02ob03re2img01.jpg", "text": "<span class=\"strong\"> Ana: </span> What are you reading? </br>\n<span class=\"strong\"> Peter: </span> This is Alan Turing's biography. I have to read it for my computer science class.</br>\n<span class=\"strong\"> Ana: </span> I see, and who is Alan Turing? </br>\n<span class=\"strong\"> Peter: </span> He was one of the many important characters in computing and software history. </br>\n<span class=\"strong\"> Ana: </span> Really? How so? </br>", "time": "100"}, {"id": 1, "image": "enpe7n1le02ob03re2img02.jpg", "text": "<span class=\"strong\"> Peter: </span> Well, hand me over your computer, I want to show you something. Look at his picture, he's one of the fathers of modern computing and I can tell you all about him. He basically conceived an idea called the Turing Machine; it involved the use of symbols in order to solve mathematical calculations. It is closely related to the development of the finite state machine.</br>\n<span class=\"strong\"> Ana: </span> Sounds difficult. I can understand only basic information on software. Please don't go into details. </br>\n<span class=\"strong\"> Peter: </span> Don't worry. Let me explain it to you in an easy way. The Turing Machine can write and read data on a tape. This machine uses symbols, similar to computer functions, in order to represent these data. For example: addition, subtraction, multiplication and some others. </br>\n<span class=\"strong\"> Ana: </span> Ok, you can go beyond with your explanation, I can understand better now. </br>\n<span class=\"strong\"> Peter: </span> Well, I guess we could say that Turing's theory helped to develop modern computers.  Do you want to know, in general terms, how the machine works? </br>", "time": "20000"}, {"id": 2, "image": "enpe7n1le02ob03re2img03.jpg", "text": "<span class=\"strong\"> Ana: </span>  Sure. I have many questions about Turing's work. </br>\n<span class=\"strong\"> Peter: </span>  Well, the machine's main function is to read and write data. It usually uses a paper tape to print cells with symbols on it. We could say that the Turing machine influenced many approaches in computing making a connection with grammar and language. </br>\n<span class=\"strong\"> Ana: </span> Really? Wow, you must have a very advanced level on software knowledge. </br>\n<span class=\"strong\"> Peter: </span>  Not really, it's just a little bit of history. Oh, I forgot to tell you that Turing also contributed to the development of the Turing Test. Computer scientists use that test when working on artificial intelligence. That invention tries to answer one of the biggest questions of our times: Can computers think? </br>\n<span class=\"strong\"> Ana: </span> That sounds interesting. </br>", "time": "97000"}], "audiosrc": "enpe7n1le02ob03re2aud01", "coverimg": "enpe7n1le02ob03re2img01.jpg"}, "type": "slideshow"}], "_data": [], "title": "", "type": "audio", "id": 1}], "type": "scholar"}], "_data": [{"contents": [{"content": "", "type": "lesson_name"}, {"content": "Alan Turing and the Turing Machine", "type": "unit_name"}, {"content": 3, "type": "position"}, {"content": "stub", "type": "origin"}, {"content": 2, "type": "lesson"}, {"content": "A1.1", "type": "level"}], "type": "metadata"}, {"contents": [{"content": "false", "type": "test"}], "type": "achievment_test"}, {"contents": [{"content": "These are words you hear and use to talk about the Turing Machine. ", "type": "text"}], "type": "target_word_describer"}, {"contents": [{"content": "Identify information about the history of software and its characteristics in simple spoken and written situations.", "type": "primary"}, {"content": "Understands key information in simple written and spoken texts about topics related to software programming and its history.  Recognizes basic ideas on programming concepts and programming history in both written and spoken passages.", "type": "secondary_1"}], "type": "objectives"}, {"content": "enpe7n1le02ob03re1img01.jpg", "type": "background_image"}, {"contents": [{"content": "a)  Look for further information about devices and computer functions derived from the concept of Turing Machine.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "b) Organize the information using some flashcards. On one side of the card, include a picture; on the other side, include basic information.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "c)\tShare your work with our classmates and instructor, and talk about it. ", "type": "text"}], "type": "takeaway_extension"}, {"contents": [{"content": "\u274f\tI look for further information about devices and computer functions related to the Turing Machine. ", "type": "text"}, {"content": "\u274f\tI design flashcards in order to organize the information I found.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "\u274f\tI talk about my work with my classmates and instructor. ", "type": "text"}, {"content": "I explain what I have written. ", "type": "text"}], "type": "takeaway_assessment"}, {"contents": [{"content": "hand over ", "type": "name"}, {"content": "to pass something to someone ", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(phrasal verb) ", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: Well, hand me over your computer. I want to show you something. Look at his picture, he's one of the fathers of modern computing.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "enpe7n1le02ob03aud01", "type": "audio"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"content": "involve ", "type": "name"}, {"content": "to cover a specific matter ", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(verb) ", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: It involved the use of symbols in order to solve mathematical calculations.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "enpe7n1le02ob03aud02", "type": "audio"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"content": "advanced ", "type": "name"}, {"content": "a high level of something ", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(adjective) ", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: Wow, you must have a very advanced level on software knowledge.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "enpe7n1le02ob03aud03", "type": "audio"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"content": "tape ", "type": "name"}, {"content": "a long piece of material where information is written ", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(noun) ", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: The Turing Machine can write and read symbols of data on a tape.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "enpe7n1le02ob03aud04", "type": "audio"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"content": "addition ", "type": "name"}, {"content": "the result of putting quantities together", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(noun) ", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example:  The Turing machine solves additions like 2 + 2 or 50064 + 48465.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "enpe7n1le02ob03aud05", "type": "audio"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"content": "go beyond ", "type": "name"}, {"content": "to go further", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(expression) ", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: Ok, you can go beyond with your explanation, I can understand better now. ", "type": "example"}, {"content": "enpe7n1le02ob03aud06", "type": "audio"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"content": "Verb Can to Talk About Ability", "type": "heading"}, {"content": "<span class=\"strong\">Modal verbs</span> have different functions. The modal verb <strong>can</strong> is useful for expressing possibility, permission, or ability. Here, we'll focus on <strong>can</strong> for talking about <strong>ability.</strong> ", "type": "describer"}, {"content": "When we talk about actions in general, we describe whether we are capable of doing those actions or not. We use <strong>can</strong> as a verb that shows that we're able to do something.</br></br>   Follow the next structure to create statements with <strong>can</strong>:</br></br>  <center>Subject + <strong>can</strong> + verb (base form) + complement</div>", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Let's take a look at the following examples:</br></br>  <i>I <strong>can</strong> talk about the history of Alan Turing. </br>She<strong> can</strong> describe the functions of the Turing Machine. </i>", "type": "text"}, {"content": "The Turing machine can write and read data on a tape.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "I can understand only basic information. Please don't go into detail.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "You can go beyond with your explanation.", "type": "example"}], "type": "learning_points"}, {"contents": [{"content": "<span class=\"strong\">Quantifiers: Many</span>", "type": "heading"}, {"content": "You use <strong>many</strong> as a quantifier of countable nouns. Remember that if a noun takes a plural form, it is likely to be a countable noun. ", "type": "describer"}, {"content": "Whenever we want to express that there is a large amount of something we can count, we use <strong>many.</strong> Please note that <span class=\"strong\">many</span> is used for elements you can count (house-houses, dog-dogs) and <span class=\"strong\">much</span> is used for elements that you cannot count (water, rice, love) .  </br></br> <strong>Many</strong> refers to a quantity above average. In order to use it in a sentence, you can follow the next structure:</br></br>  <center>Subject + verb + <strong>many+ countable noun</strong> + complement</div>", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Example:</br> <i>I have <strong>many questions</strong> about Alan Turing. Is it true that he committed suicide? I read <strong>many articles</strong> about that.</i> ", "type": "text"}, {"content": "You can also use <strong>many</strong> after <strong>there are.</strong> For example:</br></br><i><strong>There are many</strong> software developments nowadays.</i> ", "type": "text"}, {"content": "This is Alan Turing's biography. I have to read many pages of this book for my computer science class.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "He was one of the many important characters for computing and software.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "I have many questions about Turing's work.", "type": "example"}], "type": "learning_points"}, {"contents": [{"content": "How the Turing Machine Works", "type": "heading"}, {"content": "<span class=\"strong\">The Turing Machine</span> was an important development of the mathematician Alan Turing. He invented it in 1936. ", "type": "describer"}, {"content": "<strong>Some Important Facts</strong></br></br>  It's important to mention that the Turing Machine was an abstract model. It means that the nobody manufactured it. However, years after, people conceived it as a mathematical model of computation.</br></br>   The basis of Turing's idea were symbols. Thanks to those symbols, mathematical calculations (adding, subtracting or multiplying) were possible. Later on, when people were developing electronic computers, the symbols of Turing's invent turned out to be computer functions. In a nutshell, the Turing Machine was the cornerstone of computer programming.</br></br>   <strong>Some Information About How the Machine Works</strong></br></br>  We can define the Turing Machine as a mechanism that is capable of reading and writing data on a tape and it is divided into different states. Besides, It has an unlimited supply of tape that it can use as an input. First, it prints something; second, it moves to a new cell and then, it changes to a new state.</br></br>    Let's imagine the tape as an endless belt full of little holes. That endless belt can be a synonym for the memory of a computer as we know it nowadays. In other words, the belt keeps all the information. Now, each hole in the belt represents a symbol and the machine can edit the symbol or erase it. The machine can also move the symbol to the right or to the left.</br></br>   Take a look at the following image to get to know the functioning of the Turing Machine:", "type": "text"}, {"content": "enpe7n1le02ob03img01.jpg", "type": "image"}], "type": "learning_points"}], "type": "sena"}